WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.960
The first film showed a repressor moving on and off its operator.

2
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.460
A broken repressor and broken operator can both look constitutive, so how can

3
00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.260
another lac copy tell them apart?

4
00:00:15.783 --> 00:00:21.283
Geneticists place a chromosomal lac region and an F′ lac copy in one cell.

5
00:00:22.503 --> 00:00:25.683
This partial diploid is diploid only for that region.

6
00:00:27.023 --> 00:00:30.363
Each rail carries its own operator and lacZ reporter.

7
00:00:31.743 --> 00:00:35.623
Both DNA copies share the same pool of regulatory proteins.

8
00:00:36.783 --> 00:00:39.663
Read the reporter before judging the mutation.

9
00:00:40.923 --> 00:00:42.143
Find the working lacZ.

10
00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:45.323
Then check the operator beside it.

11
00:00:46.543 --> 00:00:51.223
Finally ask which repressor proteins anywhere in the cell can reach that operator.

12
00:00:52.403 --> 00:00:55.703
This order prevents a broken reporter from misleading you.

13
00:00:56.683 --> 00:01:00.683
Start with a lacI− allele that makes no working repressor.

14
00:01:01.903 --> 00:01:05.603
A normal lacI on the other rail makes red repressor protein.

15
00:01:06.843 --> 00:01:11.483
That protein diffuses through the shared cell and binds either normal operator.

16
00:01:12.683 --> 00:01:17.383
The missing product arrives from the other copy, so the defect is complemented.

17
00:01:18.783 --> 00:01:20.323
lacI− is recessive.

18
00:01:21.183 --> 00:01:23.623
Inducer can still release the repressor.

19
00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:26.627
Now change an operator itself.

20
00:01:28.067 --> 00:01:30.607
The altered orange block never leaves its rail.

21
00:01:31.887 --> 00:01:34.227
No other copy can supply a working site.

22
00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:39.247
The site cannot diffuse, so its effect stays beside linked genes.

23
00:01:40.607 --> 00:01:43.067
A linked working reporter escapes repression.

24
00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:48.127
A reporter beside the normal operator remains inducible.

25
00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:50.670
Predict this arrangement.

26
00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:55.330
The only working reporter sits beside a normal operator.

27
00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:59.550
Normal repressor comes from the other DNA rail.

28
00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:03.310
Will the reporter remain controllable by inducer?

29
00:02:09.317 --> 00:02:16.097
Yes. The normal repressor reaches the normal operator beside the working reporter.

30
00:02:17.357 --> 00:02:21.257
Without inducer, it holds transcription at a low basal level.

31
00:02:22.477 --> 00:02:25.997
With inducer, the clamp opens and the reporter turns on.

32
00:02:27.157 --> 00:02:29.497
The cell is inducible for active enzyme.

33
00:02:30.757 --> 00:02:34.257
The altered operator is linked only to a broken reporter.

34
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.620
Swap the reporters while leaving both operators fixed.

35
00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:43.460
Now the working reporter sits beside the altered operator.

36
00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:46.600
It glows with or without inducer.

37
00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:52.760
The phenotype follows the reporter's linkage to the DNA site, not a signal

38
00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:54.140
travelling across the cell.

39
00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:58.850
Notice what this test never needed: a purified repressor.

40
00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:04.690
Geneticists mapped a travelling product before anyone knew what it was made of.

41
00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:08.930
Biochemists later isolated it and found a protein.

42
00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:11.950
The genetic rules did not change.

43
00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:15.810
Partial diploids turn location into evidence.

44
00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:20.630
If an effect stays linked to one rail, suspect a control site.

45
00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:25.370
If it reaches both rails, suspect a diffusible regulator.

46
00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:31.390
This logic separates a local DNA defect from a shared cellular product.

47
00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.690
Always read the working reporter first.
